    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Episodic or coseismic uplift of anticlines above blind thrust faults indicates the important mechanical interaction between seismic events on these faults and the development of their associated\r\nfolds. High fault-tip stress concentrations are associated with coseismic slip on blind thrust faults and suggest that these structures should readily propagate to the earth’s surface. Seismic profiles of blind thrust-related earthquakes reveal diffuse zones of aftershocks surrounding the fault tip which are attributed to inelastic deformation, such as flexural-slip or extensional fracturing. The initiation and development of these features are shown to influence stress concentrations near the fault tip and fault propagation. Results obtained from a numerical model using the boundary element method show that slip along bedding planes above blind thrust faults may increase fault tip stress concentrations, as measured by the mode H stress intensity factor, and promote fault propagation. In contrast, blind thrust faults with slipping bedding planes below the fault tip show a decreased tendency to propagate. Our results suggest that faults located at shallow depths in the earth’s crust may exhibit both an increased tendency to propagate as a result of interaction with the earth’s surface and slip along bedding planes above the fault tip and a decreased tendency to propagate due to slip along bedding planes below the fault tip. The complex interaction between bedding-plane slip and other forms of inelastic deformation above and below the fault tip may control the evolution of blind thrust systems. The distribution of Coulomb stresses around blind thrust faults supports the stress intensity factor analysis; significant thrust fault propagation is encouraged by slip along bedding planes above the fault and discouraged by bedding plane slip below the tip of the thrust fault. Our results demonstrate that the magnitude and style of inelastic deformation in active fault systems influence fault propagation and have important implications for the assessment of seismic hazards.",
